WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.048 --> 00:00:16.453
In a neighborhood west of Washington Square on Manhattan Island in New York City, the streets run a

2
00:00:16.493 --> 00:00:17.194
little crazy.

3
00:00:17.855 --> 00:00:24.020
They break up into small strips called "places," and these "places" have strange angles and curves.

4
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:30.536
Streets in these "places" seem to begin at one point and then end abruptly, only to begin again

5
00:00:30.657 --> 00:00:32.037
someplace else entirely!

6
00:00:32.777 --> 00:00:36.919
An artist quickly discovered what a wonderful asset living there would be.

7
00:00:37.619 --> 00:00:42.862
He said to himself, "Think of all the bill collectors who would get lost here trying to find

8
00:00:42.922 --> 00:00:45.943
some poor artist who owed money for paints and paper!"

9
00:00:46.743 --> 00:00:53.766
This neighborhood became quaint old Greenwich Village, and the artists soon arrived looking for big windows full of

10
00:00:53.846 --> 00:00:59.676
natural light, eighteenth-century gables, high-ceilinged attics, and low rents.

11
00:01:00.757 --> 00:01:07.260
So many artists streamed into Greenwich Village over time that it soon stopped being a "place" and became

12
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.421
a "colony," an artist's colony filled with artists' studios.

13
00:01:12.382 --> 00:01:19.685
At the top of one three-story brick building, Sue and Johnsy (a nickname for Joanna) had their studio.

14
00:01:20.525 --> 00:01:26.588
Johnsy was from California and Sue was from Maine, and they had met in a small restaurant on

15
00:01:26.708 --> 00:01:27.388
Eighth Street.

16
00:01:28.272 --> 00:01:34.454
Over dinner, they found they had so much in common about food, fashion, and art that they decided

17
00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:36.014
to open a studio together.

18
00:01:37.034 --> 00:01:44.096
That was in May, but in November, a cold, invisible killer appeared and stalked the colony.

19
00:01:44.977 --> 00:01:51.378
The doctors called the killer pneumonia, an infection of the lungs, and it touched one person here and

20
00:01:51.438 --> 00:01:52.159
another there.

21
00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:58.721
On the Lower East Side of Manhattan, where most of the poor people lived in tenements, this disease

22
00:01:58.821 --> 00:02:01.463
struck boldly and claimed many victims.

23
00:02:02.744 --> 00:02:11.450
Fortunately, the maze of narrow and moss-grown neighborhoods west of Washington Square remained fairly safe, but only for

24
00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:11.950
a while.

25
00:02:12.991 --> 00:02:18.374
Soon, the little woman named Johnsy from sunny California came down with pneumonia.

26
00:02:19.335 --> 00:02:27.104
She was no match for the disease, so Johnsy lay, scarcely moving on her painted iron bedstead, looking

27
00:02:27.144 --> 00:02:31.025
through the small windowpanes at the blank side of the next brick house.

28
00:02:32.146 --> 00:02:38.327
One morning, a busy doctor who had just visited Johnsy in her bedroom invited Sue into the hallway.

29
00:02:38.347 --> 00:02:44.889
"She has one chance in ten of surviving this illness," he said, as he shook down the mercury

30
00:02:44.989 --> 00:02:45.889
in his thermometer.

31
00:02:45.909 --> 00:02:51.051
"And the only thing that will give her that chance is for her to want to live.

32
00:02:51.811 --> 00:02:55.572
For some reason, your friend has made up her mind that she's not going to get well.

33
00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:57.661
Has she anything on her mind?"

34
00:02:58.502 --> 00:03:02.764
"She she wanted to paint the Bay of Naples some day," said Sue.

35
00:03:02.784 --> 00:03:05.366
"Paint?" said the doctor.

36
00:03:05.386 --> 00:03:06.527
"Is that all?

37
00:03:07.127 --> 00:03:09.949
Doesn't she have anything else more important on her mind?

38
00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:11.911
A man, for instance?"

39
00:03:13.291 --> 00:03:14.032
"A man?"

40
00:03:14.652 --> 00:03:15.893
said Sue, sharply.

41
00:03:15.913 --> 00:03:18.535
"Is a man worth more than her art?

42
00:03:19.516 --> 00:03:22.057
No, doctor, there is no man."

43
00:03:22.077 --> 00:03:26.952
"Well, I will do all that science can accomplish," said the doctor.

44
00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:33.375
"But whenever a patient begins to count the carriages in her funeral procession, the odds of survival go

45
00:03:33.395 --> 00:03:39.577
down. If you get her to ask one question about the new women's fashions, I will promise you

46
00:03:39.637 --> 00:03:42.999
a one-in-five chance for her, instead of a one-in-ten."

47
00:03:43.779 --> 00:03:49.902
After the doctor had gone, Sue went into the studio and cried until her handkerchief was dripping wet.

48
00:03:50.982 --> 00:03:57.493
Then she got herself together and swaggered into Johnsy's bedroom with her drawing pad, whistling a popular tune.

49
00:03:58.433 --> 00:04:03.596
Johnsy lay, making hardly a ripple under the covers, with her face toward the window.

50
00:04:04.257 --> 00:04:06.698
Sue stopped whistling, thinking she was asleep.

51
00:04:07.338 --> 00:04:12.341
Then, Sue sat down and began to work on an illustration she had been hired to draw for

52
00:04:12.361 --> 00:04:13.402
a magazine story.

53
00:04:14.082 --> 00:04:18.865
"Young artists must make money on their way to making real art," Sue said to herself.

54
00:04:18.885 --> 00:04:24.008
"Just as young authors write magazine stories before they can write great literature."

55
00:04:24.784 --> 00:04:30.888
As Sue was sketching a pair of elegant horse-riding pants and a figure of an Idaho cowboy, she

56
00:04:30.948 --> 00:04:33.769
heard a low sound, repeated several times.

57
00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:36.211
She went quickly to the bedside.

58
00:04:36.911 --> 00:04:42.554
Johnsy's eyes were wide open, and she was looking out the window and counting—backward.

59
00:04:43.655 --> 00:04:51.899
"Twelve," she said, and a little later, "eleven"; and then "ten," and "nine"; and then "eight" and "seven,"

60
00:04:52.059 --> 00:04:52.840
almost together.

61
00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:55.862
Sue looked anxiously out of the window.

62
00:04:55.882 --> 00:04:57.563
"What is she counting?"

63
00:04:58.223 --> 00:04:59.224
she said quietly.

64
00:04:59.864 --> 00:05:05.368
There was only a bare, dreary yard to be seen, and the blank side of the brick house

65
00:05:05.448 --> 00:05:06.349
twenty feet away.

66
00:05:07.309 --> 00:05:13.453
An old ivy vine, gnarled and decayed at the roots, climbed halfway up the brick wall.

67
00:05:14.294 --> 00:05:21.179
The cold breath of autumn had pulled the leaves from the vine until its skeleton branches clung, almost

68
00:05:21.239 --> 00:05:23.000
bare, to the crumbling bricks.

69
00:05:23.916 --> 00:05:24.697
"What is it, dear?"

70
00:05:25.277 --> 00:05:25.958
cried Sue.

71
00:05:25.978 --> 00:05:29.901
"Six," said Johnsy, in almost a whisper.

72
00:05:30.702 --> 00:05:32.123
"They're falling faster now.

73
00:05:32.904 --> 00:05:35.005
Three days ago there were almost a hundred.

74
00:05:35.646 --> 00:05:37.267
It made my head ache to count them.

75
00:05:38.128 --> 00:05:39.149
But now it's easy.

76
00:05:39.729 --> 00:05:40.730
There goes another one.

77
00:05:41.311 --> 00:05:42.972
There are only five left now."

78
00:05:42.992 --> 00:05:44.994
"Five what, dear?

79
00:05:45.134 --> 00:05:46.635
Tell me," said Sue.

80
00:05:46.655 --> 00:05:48.837
"Leaves, on the ivy vine.

81
00:05:49.398 --> 00:05:52.040
When the last one falls, I must go too.

82
00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:54.220
I've known that for three days.

83
00:05:54.981 --> 00:05:56.241
Didn't the doctor tell you?"

84
00:05:56.261 --> 00:06:01.062
"Oh, I've never heard such nonsense," said Sue, scornfully.

85
00:06:01.082 --> 00:06:05.204
"What have old ivy leaves got to do with your getting well?

86
00:06:05.904 --> 00:06:08.505
And you used to love that vine so much, Johnsy.

87
00:06:08.705 --> 00:06:09.465
Don't be silly.

88
00:06:10.225 --> 00:06:15.147
Why, the doctor told me this morning that your chances for getting well soon were very good!

89
00:06:16.107 --> 00:06:20.876
Try to sip some chicken broth now, and let me go back to my work, so I can

90
00:06:20.956 --> 00:06:26.359
sell the illustration and buy some wine for my sick friend, and pork chops for my greedy self."

91
00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:32.643
"You don't need to get any more wine," said Johnsy, keeping her eyes fixed out the window.

92
00:06:32.663 --> 00:06:34.304
"There goes another.

93
00:06:35.064 --> 00:06:37.085
No, I don't want any broth.

94
00:06:37.746 --> 00:06:38.986
That leaves just four.

95
00:06:39.707 --> 00:06:42.688
I want to see the last one fall before it gets dark.

96
00:06:43.289 --> 00:06:44.509
Then I'll go too."

97
00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:52.891
"Johnsy, dear," said Sue, bending over her, "will you promise me to keep your eyes closed, and not

98
00:06:52.971 --> 00:06:55.093
look out the window until I am done working?

99
00:06:56.094 --> 00:06:58.275
I must hand those drawings in by tomorrow.

100
00:06:58.856 --> 00:07:01.158
I need the light, or I would draw with the shade down."

101
00:07:01.178 --> 00:07:03.279
"Couldn't you draw in the other room?"

102
00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.121
asked Johnsy, coldly.

103
00:07:05.141 --> 00:07:08.504
"I'd rather be here by you," said Sue.

104
00:07:08.524 --> 00:07:13.568
"Besides, I don't want you to keep looking at those silly ivy leaves."

105
00:07:14.656 --> 00:07:19.880
"Tell me as soon as you have finished," said Johnsy, closing her eyes, and lying white and still

106
00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:24.044
as a fallen statue, "because I want to see the last one fall.

107
00:07:24.604 --> 00:07:25.725
I'm tired of waiting.

108
00:07:26.165 --> 00:07:27.346
I'm tired of thinking.

109
00:07:28.167 --> 00:07:34.592
I want to let go of my hold on everything and go sailing down, down, just like one

110
00:07:34.632 --> 00:07:36.434
of those poor, tired leaves."

111
00:07:37.214 --> 00:07:38.976
"Try to sleep," said Sue.

112
00:07:38.996 --> 00:07:45.598
"I've got another magazine illustration to do—an old hermit miner—and I must go get Mr.

113
00:07:45.658 --> 00:07:46.999
Behrman to be my model.

114
00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:48.961
I'll not be gone a minute.

115
00:07:49.481 --> 00:07:51.263
Don't try to move until I come back."
